Third-party software in binary releases
---------------------------------------

The release bundles built from this repository also contain:

  - Go modules statically linked into the binaries built from manager/ (listed
    in manager/go.mod), under the MIT, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause and ISC
    licences.
  - MediaMTX (https://github.com/bluenviron/mediamtx), MIT licence, unmodified
    official binary (fetched by build/mediamtx.sh; its licence is shipped as
    mediamtx.LICENSE).
  - srtla_send from BELABOX/srtla (https://github.com/BELABOX/srtla),
    AGPL-3.0. It is built from a pinned upstream commit with one change, applied
    by build/srtla.sh (the local socket listens on 127.0.0.1 instead of all
    interfaces). That script and the pinned commit are its corresponding source.
  - srtla_send_rs, the SRTLA sender of irlserver/srtla_send (https://github.com/irlserver/srtla_send), MIT
    licence, with the Rust crates it links statically (MIT, Apache-2.0 and similar permissive licences, each
    shipped with the release). It is built from a pinned upstream commit with one change, applied by
    build/srtla-rs.sh (the local socket listens on 127.0.0.1 instead of all interfaces).
  - ristsender from librist (https://code.videolan.org/rist/librist), BSD-2-Clause licence, statically linked
    with the copies of mbedtls (Apache-2.0), cJSON (MIT) and LZ4 (BSD-2-Clause) it bundles. It is built without
    changes from a pinned release by build/rist.sh.
  - BlueALSA (bluez-alsa, https://github.com/arkq/bluez-alsa), MIT licence, built by build/bluealsa.sh, with
    libsbc (LGPL-2.1-or-later) linked statically into its daemon; the release ships libsbc's source.
  - srt-live-transmit and the libsrt shared library from Haivision SRT
    (https://github.com/Haivision/srt), Mozilla Public License 2.0. They are
    built without changes from a pinned upstream tag by build/srt.sh; the
    release ships their licence and where their source is.

The engine links dynamically against GStreamer and NVIDIA L4T libraries that
are part of the board's operating system; they are not distributed here.


